// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: capability, attenuation, records, soundness
// @expected-error: E313
// @skip: T0918: widening is not refused. `subtype.rs` carries the correct rule and nothing asks it; a gate in check_expr was written and measured INERT because every binding reports the plain base type. Remedy: establish whether the binding erases the capability or the diagnostic renderer does — they need different fixes.
// @description: The negative pole — a value carrying fewer capabilities
//   may NOT be passed where more are required.
//
// The companion to `capability_on_a_record_keeps_its_fields`. That file
// checks a restricted record is still a record; this one checks the
// restriction still means something.
//
// Attenuation is one-way. Handing over `[Read, Write]` where `[Read]` is
// wanted is safe — the callee simply uses less than it was given. The
// reverse is the whole point of the feature: a caller holding a
// read-only view must not be able to reach a function that writes,
// because that is precisely the confinement the type was chosen to
// express.
//
// WHY THE POLE IS LOAD-BEARING HERE IN PARTICULAR: before the field-access
// fix, BOTH directions failed, and both failed with the same E103 about
// a non-record type. So there was no program that told a working
// attenuation check apart from a broken one — the feature could have
// been doing nothing at all and every spec in this directory would have
// stayed green. A relaxation that removes the check and a fix that
// repairs it are indistinguishable from the passing side; only a
// program that must be REFUSED separates them.
//
// The refusal must also be about CAPABILITIES. If this file starts
// failing with E103 again, the fix regressed even though the test still
// reports a failure — which is why the expected code is pinned.

public type Store is { n: Int };

/// Requires the right to write.
public fn bump(s: &mut Store with [Read, Write]) {
    s.n = s.n + 1;
}

/// Holds only the right to read, and tries to write anyway.
public fn sneak(ro: &mut Store with [Read]) {
    // ERROR: `[Read]` is not `[Read, Write]`. Rights can be given away,
    // never taken back.
    bump(ro);
}

fn main() {
    let mut s = Store { n: 1 };
    sneak(&mut s);
    print(f"{s.n}");
}
